WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.255
[SINGING]

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:07.780
One day, I got a call
from Evan Ziporyn--

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:12.160
a friend of mine and colleague
and composer at MIT--

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.080
and he said that
he was approached

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.890
by Sanjay Sarma of
Open Learning to see

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:21.370
if he could create some
kind of musical experience

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:23.500
for MIT's virtual commencement.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.592
[SINGING]

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.530
Just to fool around
with it, I just kind of

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:34.720
wrote something really quickly--

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.420
just as a basic
proof of concept,

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:39.580
like, how about
something like this?

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.502
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.970
And sent it to [? Eran. ?] And
he went, oh, that's perfect.

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.220
Let's just use that--

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:53.970
which never happens.

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:56.780
It was really important
to me that we could

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.940
have every person contribute.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:00.870
And of course, this
is a musical project,

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.407
which means that probably,
people who are musicians

00:01:03.407 --> 00:01:04.865
are going to feel
more comfortable.

00:01:04.865 --> 00:01:07.994
[SINGING]

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:13.340
But we really wanted everyone to
feel comfortable contributing.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:18.110
And so, we did an analysis of
all of the notes in the piece

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:21.110
that Evan wrote, and figured
out which ones would be harder

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:23.840
to sing, but which ones
might be easier to sing.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.390
And so, on from the
submissions website,

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:29.597
anyone can pick their level
of comfort or experience.

00:01:29.597 --> 00:01:31.430
And then the system
automatically gives them

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:33.750
notes to sing that are
appropriate for their comfort

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:34.250
level.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:37.000
[SINGING]

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:42.530
For everyone who
submitted out there,

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.530
you don't really have to
worry about whether you

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:45.883
were singing exactly in tune.

00:01:45.883 --> 00:01:47.050
We kind of fixed it for you.

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:49.870
[SINGING]

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:02.040
OK.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.750
Hey everyone.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:05.610
Getting the team together
to make it happen

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.910
was essential, and was,
quintessentially, MIT.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:12.360
Because I think MIT is one
of the few places where

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.790
you think of a project
like this and you go,

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:17.820
OK, we're going to
need a composer working

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:18.900
with Joel Roston.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.780
We're going to need
fantastic audio people.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.750
We're going to need the most
amazing coder on the planet.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:29.150
We're going to need
fantastic web developers.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:33.570
We're going to need
fantastic video people.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.955
We're going to need fantastic
communications people who

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:37.680
will get the word out.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.890
And all these people are a phone
call away or an email away,

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:43.950
and will answer.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:47.368
And everybody is devoted to MIT.

00:02:47.368 --> 00:02:47.910
Close to you.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:49.620
But if the faces are
still coming out--

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.570
And I still remember
the first meeting,

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.210
when we started to
get people together.

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:55.140
And we're talking
about the website

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.672
that we would need to get
the input from participants.

00:02:58.672 --> 00:02:59.880
And we're all going, oh, god.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:01.050
That sounds so complicated.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:03.480
That's going to take two
weeks just to get that up.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.580
And Ike, just very
softly, says, I'll

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.370
have it ready by
2:00 PM tomorrow.

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:09.480
And then he did.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.330
One of the neat things is
we had this feedback box.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:16.320
And we intended this to be this
piece of the website did not

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:17.510
work well.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:19.860
But actually, instead,
in the feedback box

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.860
we started getting these
comments like, hey, man.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:24.180
This is awesome.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:28.220
Way to go, [? Eran. ?]
Hooray for MIT.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:32.730
It was incredible to see that
people really did contribute.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:35.970
And you could see,
every minute or so--

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.160
every 30 seconds
or so at the peak--

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:39.930
there'd be another
clip showing up.

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:44.990
[MUSICAL SOUNDS]

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:48.730
One of the really
wonderful things I noticed

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:50.800
was that people
were being creative

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:54.035
with how they submitted.

00:03:54.035 --> 00:03:55.835
[MUSICAL SOUNDS]

00:04:02.085 --> 00:04:02.585
Ti.

00:04:02.585 --> 00:04:05.450
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:07.970
And we also saw a lot of
families coming together.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.850
[SINGING]

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:20.670
And it was really
wonderful to see

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:23.100
a sense of community,
even when we were just

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.530
asking for a single note.

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:27.729
[SINGING]

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.320
If this were just a
song that was sung,

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:35.875
it wouldn't be quite as
interesting or as challenging.

00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:39.070
A really fascinating
part of this project

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:42.160
has been the effort of
putting the video together

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:42.800
with the music.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.100
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:48.350
The visuals for the piece
were left really open ended,

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:49.900
which was wonderful.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:54.000
And the idea of sort of
a journey came to mind.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.070
The idea of sort of
creating this journey

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:59.430
that the student takes every
day as part of this piece,

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:04.140
really, I thought resonated
with this idea of students going

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:07.310
through their commencement and
through their actual academic

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:07.810
journey.

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:10.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.520
There's a part of the
piece that I wrote where

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:15.130
the music kind of ascends.

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:17.435
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.238
When I wrote it, I wasn't
thinking of anything.

00:05:21.238 --> 00:05:23.488
It was just something I felt
that was needed musically

00:05:23.488 --> 00:05:24.110
at that time.

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:27.110
But what was really magic
to me was when Clayton first

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:30.610
showed us that moment that
the camera crosses Mass Ave

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:33.510
and would walk up the stairs
into the infinite corridor.

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:35.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:43.180
And it mashes the music
absolutely perfectly,

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:47.190
and it made the music work.

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:51.310
It got-- it was the
magical moment for me.

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:56.520
That's the real sign to me of
a great collaboration, when

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:58.680
you do what you do
as well as you can,

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:01.590
and then somebody else
does what they do.

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:04.050
And they take what
you've done and turn it

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.470
into something bigger than
what you thought it could be.

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:08.700
There was this discussion
early on about trying

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:12.360
to hide some MIT Easter
eggs inside the piece.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.083
At the time, I was
thinking, you know

00:06:14.083 --> 00:06:15.750
it'd be good just to
get the piece done.

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:18.110
Let's worry about
Easter eggs later on.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:21.792
But we did put four different
moments inside the piece.

00:06:21.792 --> 00:06:23.250
And we're excited
that people might

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:25.350
find them and share those.

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:30.120
I think the most heartwarming
part of this project to me

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:33.240
is the fact that it
really is the voices

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.400
of the graduates of 2020.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.950
And these are MIT
students who have

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:40.290
put their heart and
their expression

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:42.540
into this musical piece.

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:46.410
And therefore, this is really
a creation of the students,

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:48.930
by the students, and
for the students.

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:52.280
[MUSIC PLAYING]

